## Q4

Let
$$
X =
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 4 \\
2 & 5 \\
3 & 6
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

a. Write down $X^\top$ and state its dimensions. [2 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
Switching the rows and columns of $X$ gives
$$
X^\top=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 2 & 3\\
4 & 5 & 6
\end{bmatrix}.
$$
$X^\top$ has 2 rows and 3 columns, so its dimensions are $2 \times 3$.

:::

b. Compute $X^\top X$ and $XX^\top$ by hand. Explain why these two products have different dimensions even though both involve $X$ and $X^\top$. [4 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
Using row-by-column multiplication,
$$
X^\top X=
\begin{bmatrix}
1^2+2^2+3^2 & 1(4)+2(5)+3(6)\\
4(1)+5(2)+6(3) & 4^2+5^2+6^2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
14 & 32\\
32 & 77
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Similarly,
$$
XX^\top=
\begin{bmatrix}
1(1)+4(4) & 1(2)+4(5) & 1(3)+4(6)\\
2(1)+5(4) & 2(2)+5(5) & 2(3)+5(6)\\
3(1)+6(4) & 3(2)+6(5) & 3(3)+6(6)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
17 & 22 & 27\\
22 & 29 & 36\\
27 & 36 & 45
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

$X$ is $3 \times 2$ and $X^\top$ is $2 \times 3$.
Therefore, $X^\top X$ has dimensions $(2 \times3)(3 \times 2)
= 2 \times 2$, while $XX^\top$ has dimensions
$(3 \times 2)(2 \times 3) = 3 \times 3$.
The result keeps the number of rows of the first matrix and
the number of columns of the second matrix.
:::

c. Let $\mathbf{v}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\2\end{bmatrix}$. Compute $\mathbf{v}^\top\mathbf{v}$ and $\mathbf{v}\mathbf{v}^\top$ by hand, and state the dimension of each result. [4 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
For $v^\top v$, I multiply the row vector by the column vector:
$$
v^\top v=
\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\2\end{bmatrix}
=1^2+(-1)^2+2^2=6.
$$
The result has dimensions $1 \times 1$.

For $vv^\top$, I multiply the column vector by the row vector:
$$
vv^\top=
\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\2\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & -1 & 2\\
-1 & 1 & -2\\
2 & -2 & 4
\end{bmatrix}.
$$
The result has dimensions $3 \times 3$.

:::

