## Q6 

a. Define, in your own words, what it means for a set of vectors to be linearly independent. [1 mark]

:::{.callout-tip}
We combine the vectors using coefficients. If the vectors are
linearly independent, the combination equals the zero vector
only when all coefficients are zero.

:::

b. Consider
    $$
   \mathbf{x}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\1\end{bmatrix},\quad
   \mathbf{x}_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\\1\end{bmatrix},\quad
   \mathbf{x}_3=\begin{bmatrix}2\\5\\3\end{bmatrix}.
    $$ 
Show that $\mathbf{x}_1,\mathbf{x}_2,\mathbf{x}_3$ are linearly dependent by finding constants $c_1,c_2,c_3$, not all zero, such that $c_1\mathbf{x}_1+c_2\mathbf{x}_2+c_3\mathbf{x}_3=\mathbf{0}$. [3 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
I choose $c_1=2$, $c_2=1$, and $c_3=-1$. Then
$$
2\mathbf{x}_1+\mathbf{x}_2-\mathbf{x}_3
=
\begin{bmatrix}2\\4\\2\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\\1\end{bmatrix}
-
\begin{bmatrix}2\\5\\3\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}.
$$
These coefficients are not all zero. Therefore, the three vectors
are linearly dependent.


:::


c. Let $M=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{x}_1 & \mathbf{x}_2 & \mathbf{x}_3\end{bmatrix}$. What is $\operatorname{rank}(M)$? Explain your reasoning using your answer to part (b). [2 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
From part (b), $\mathbf{x}_3=2\mathbf{x}_1+\mathbf{x}_2$.
So the third column depends on the first two.

$\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\mathbf{x}_2$ are not multiples of each other.
They are linearly independent. Therefore, $\operatorname{rank}(M)=2$.

