## Q10 

Let $\mathbf{X}=\begin{bmatrix}X_1\\X_2\\X_3\end{bmatrix}$ be a random vector
with
$$
\boldsymbol{\mu}=E[\mathbf{X}]=\begin{bmatrix}2\\0\\-1\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\Sigma=\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{X})=
\begin{bmatrix}
4 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 2 & -1\\
0 & -1 & 3
\end{bmatrix}.
$$
Let $A=\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0\\ 0 & 1 & -1\end{bmatrix}$ and
$\mathbf{b}=\begin{bmatrix}5\\-2\end{bmatrix}$, and define
$\mathbf{Y}=A\mathbf{X}+\mathbf{b}$.


a. State $E[\mathbf{Y}]$ and $\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{Y})$, citing the
   properties used. [3 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
By the linearity of expectation,
$$
E[\mathbf{Y}]
=E[A\mathbf{X}+\mathbf{b}]
=A E[\mathbf{X}]+\mathbf{b}
=A\boldsymbol{\mu}+\mathbf{b}.
$$

Adding a constant vector does not change the covariance matrix.
For a linear transformation,
$$
\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{Y})
=\operatorname{Var}(A\mathbf{X}+\mathbf{b})
=A\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{X})A^\top
=A\Sigma A^\top.
$$
:::

b. Compute $E[\mathbf{Y}]$ by hand. [2 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
I substitute $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ into the formula:
$$
E[\mathbf{Y}]
=A\boldsymbol{\mu}+\mathbf{b}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 1 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
0\\
-1
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
5\\
-2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
5\\
-2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
7\\
-1
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

:::

c. Compute $\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{Y})=A\Sigma A^\top$ by hand, showing
   your intermediate matrix products. [3 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
First, calculate $A\Sigma$:
$$
A\Sigma=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 1 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
4 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 2 & -1\\
0 & -1 & 3
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
5 & 3 & -1\\
1 & 3 & -4
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Then multiply by $A^\top$:
$$
\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{Y})
=A\Sigma A^\top
=
\begin{bmatrix}
5 & 3 & -1\\
1 & 3 & -4
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0\\
1 & 1\\
0 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
5+3 & 3+1\\
1+3 & 3+4
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
8 & 4\\
4 & 7
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

:::

d. Let $\mathbf{a}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\2\end{bmatrix}$. Compute
   $\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{a}^\top\mathbf{X})$ using the appropriate
   property, without recomputing $\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{X})$ from
   scratch. [3 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
The variance of a linear combination is
$$
\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{a}^\top\mathbf{X})
=\mathbf{a}^\top\Sigma\mathbf{a}.
$$

First,
$$
\Sigma\mathbf{a}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
4 & 1 & 0\\
1 & 2 & -1\\
0 & -1 & 3
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\-1\\2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
3\\-3\\7
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Therefore,
$$
\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{a}^\top\mathbf{X})
=
\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}3\\-3\\7\end{bmatrix}
=3+3+14=20.
$$
:::

e. In R, using `MASS::mvrnorm()`, draw 100,000 observations from a
   distribution with mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ and variance $\Sigma$ (you may
   treat $\mathbf{X}$ as multivariate normal for this simulation). Confirm
   your answers to (b), (c), and (d) using `colMeans()` and `cov()`. [3 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
I simulate 100,000 observations and compare the sample results
with my calculations.

```{r}
set.seed(3201)

mu <- c(2, 0, -1)

