## Q11

a. Suppose $\mathbf{X}\sim N_3(\boldsymbol{\mu},\Sigma)$ using the
   $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ and $\Sigma$ from Question 10. What is the marginal
   distribution of $X_2$ alone? Give its distribution and parameters. [2 marks]

:::{.callout-tip}
Each component of a multivariate normal vector is normally distributed.
For $X_2$, the mean is $\mu_2=0$ and the variance is $\Sigma_{22}=2$.
Therefore,
$$
X_2 \sim N(0,2),
$$
where 2 is the variance. The standard deviation is $\sqrt{2}$.

:::

c. Using property 4 of the multivariate normal (zero covariance implies
   independence), determine whether $X_1$ and $X_3$ are independent.
   Justify your answer using an entry of $\Sigma$. [1 mark]

:::{.callout-tip}
The covariance between $X_1$ and $X_3$ is $\Sigma_{13}=0$.
They are jointly normal. For jointly normal variables,
zero covariance implies independence.
Therefore, $X_1$ and $X_3$ are independent.
:::

